  {
    "id": "10.1038/s44159-026-00604-8",
    "venue": "nrpsychol",
    "year": 2026,
    "track": null,
    "title": "Machine learning of digital traces to detect risk for behavioural addictions online",
    "authors": [
      "Tyrone L. Burleigh",
      "Yifei Hao",
      "Bruno Schivinski",
      "Maria Prokofieva",
      "Daniel Zarate",
      "Taylor Brown",
      "Vasileios Stavropoulos"
    ],
    "author_ids": [
      "https://orcid.org/0009-0002-7510-2557",
      "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6964-4662"
    ],
    "abstract": "Problematic engagement with online activities (such as problematic smartphone or social media use, or gaming disorders and related clinically relevant conditions) have become an important concern in digital mental health. Because these behaviours unfold through interactions with digital devices and platforms, they also generate data that can be used as behavioural traces that inform clinicians about potential risks. In this Review, we synthesize empirical and conceptual work on how digital trace data and machine learning can support early risk detection and prioritize individuals who show evidence of problematic online engagement and related behavioural addictions. Machine learning approaches that analyse information such as how people move through apps, the timing and structure of their online sessions, how frequently they switch between activities, the way they type and the language they use online are promising methods in this field, but analyses and interpretation must be conducted in relation to established psychological models of addiction and ethical and regulatory guidelines. Future progress will depend on the availability of larger and more diverse datasets, clearer validation against clinically meaningful outcomes, and governance frameworks that protect privacy, fairness and user autonomy.",
    "keywords": [
      "Psychology",
      "Interdisciplinary studies",
      "Psychiatric disorders"
    ],
